                        ],
                        "form": "keine Zufallsvariablen"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f57f22b9-e2cc-3e9f-abde-18e6f05e2e58",
                        "entry_id": "efeeafe5-1a9c-3913-8864-e3ac460049b4",
                        "glosses": [
                            "eine Größe, deren Wert vom Zufall abhängig ist"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Statistik"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "20451e26-b85c-4398-822e-4eaf4b3ccdfd",
                                "sense_id": "f57f22b9-e2cc-3e9f-abde-18e6f05e2e58",
                                "text": "Die Anzahl der an einem Tag in einem Ort versandten Briefe ist eine Zufallsvariable."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "35c6ebc6-56ae-4615-8423-e55f7a1cbd75",
                                "sense_id": "f57f22b9-e2cc-3e9f-abde-18e6f05e2e58",
                                "text": "Das, was wir intuitiv vermuten würden, ist tatsächlich wahr: Wenn wir Funktionen unabhängiger Zufallsvariabler bilden, sind diese wieder unabhängig."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4f094be0-1af8-48e3-864a-0c5c3035bf1d",
                                "sense_id": "f57f22b9-e2cc-3e9f-abde-18e6f05e2e58",
                                "text": "Die Wahrscheinlichkeitstheorie geht davon aus, daß die Verteilung einer Zufallsvariablen bekannt ist."
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "a6874f83-9c55-4475-aa09-3edefd6ffb12",
                        "source_entry_id": "efeeafe5-1a9c-3913-8864-e3ac460049b4",
                        "source_sense_id": "f57f22b9-e2cc-3e9f-abde-18e6f05e2e58",
                        "source_sense_description": "Mathematik, Statistik: eine Größe, deren Wert vom Zufall abhängig ist",
                        "target_lang_word": "random variable",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "random variable",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "eec2a972-c48a-34b3-9a65-8da5c86d4518",
                                        "entry_id": "902ad22f-8636-3fe5-a908-4224a3ba211a",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A quantity whose value is random and to which a probability distribution is assigned, such as the possible outcome of a roll of a dice."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1d59f0c6-67c1-364d-a1ed-136ab818ee78",
                                        "entry_id": "902ad22f-8636-3fe5-a908-4224a3ba211a",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A measurable function from a sample space to the measurable space of possible values of the variable."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "formal"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "304ed58a-a38c-4715-8e70-4622dbdc5588",
                                                "sense_id": "1d59f0c6-67c1-364d-a1ed-136ab818ee78",
                                                "text": "Henceforth the symbol X(w) will be used for the random variable X#58;S#92;rightarrow#92;mathbb#123;R#125;."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "336e7179-a662-452b-a6a6-fce311a03aa6",
                                                "sense_id": "1d59f0c6-67c1-364d-a1ed-136ab818ee78",
                                                "text": "The particular example considered here is the Hilbert space of random variables."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "83e7453d-ac1e-43ba-a86f-353644efc2ad",
                                                "sense_id": "1d59f0c6-67c1-364d-a1ed-136ab818ee78",
                                                "text": "A two-dimensional random variable is a mapping of the points in the sample space to ordered pairs {x, y}. Usually, when dealing with a pair of random variables, the sample space naturally partitions itself so that it can be viewed as a combination of two simpler sample spaces."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
